% ------------------------------------------------------------
\begin{abstract}
Geopolitical developments can affect firms and markets through multiple, time-varying exposure channels, yet monitoring workflows must connect event records, reporting, economic entities, and market data before analysts can assess those channels. This paper examines whether a prototype designed for future live geopolitical monitoring can exercise parts of that workflow on archived data. We replay six bounded 2024-Q1 cases spanning four geopolitical strata---conflict and shipping disruption, sanctions and export controls, and commodity supply shocks---using GDELT event records, GDELT Global Knowledge Graph (GKG) version~1 news metadata, direct source-URL matching, metadata-based lexical retrieval, rule-based sector labels, and descriptive market-model calculations. Two cases are examined in depth: in the 12~January Red Sea case, 30 event records were linked to 31 same-source rows; lexical retrieval returned 230 candidates for 25 events, but only one exact-source pair appeared among those candidates. In the 12~March semiconductor export-controls case, the source article ranked 4{,}033rd under a baseline metadata query and 55th after query enrichment. Four additional cases---Iran oil sanctions, China rare-earth export curbs, Russia Black Sea energy disruption, and India pharma API supply shock---follow the same pre-specified inclusion protocol and report descriptive event-study results. A geographic propagation map traces each case from its event origin through directly affected countries to the Indian sectoral market outcome. Researcher-defined market baskets produced descriptive cumulative abnormal-return estimates, but neither basket assignment nor the estimates establish exposure or event causality. The replay demonstrates that archived records can be processed and traceable source links retained across varied event types; it also exposes weak retrieval and unresolved exposure validation. A longer 2022--2024 daily panel supporting Toda--Yamamoto Granger analysis is left for future work. Code and outputs are publicly available at \url{https://github.com/krish20041610/GeoSignal}. The results do not establish live operational performance, semantic retrieval accuracy, predictive validity, or causal market effects.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} geopolitical risk; event data; news retrieval; historical replay; event study; exposure mapping

% ------------------------------------------------------------
\section{Introduction}\label{sec:intro}

Geopolitical events can reach firms and financial markets through trade restrictions, conflict, transport disruption, commodity supply, and other channels. Assessing those pathways from unfolding information requires more than detecting an event: an analytical workflow must connect structured event records to reporting, identify potentially exposed economic entities, and place those hypotheses alongside market observations. Geopolitical-risk indices and event datasets provide established ways to represent parts of this problem, while financial event studies examine market responses around dated information arrivals~\cite{CaldaraIacoviello2022,MacKinlay1997}. The remaining practical challenge addressed here is how to assemble these stages into a traceable monitoring workflow and to evaluate what a prototype can do with historical inputs.

We study GeoSignal, a prototype architecture intended for future live geopolitical monitoring. The empirical exercise is an offline replay using archived records; it is not a live deployment. We ask:

\begin{quote}
\emph{To what extent can the prototype connect historical geopolitical event records, news metadata, candidate economic exposures, and market context, and where do those connections fail?}
\end{quote}

This question focuses the paper on workflow feasibility and evidence quality rather than stock-impact prediction.

The replay covers \textbf{six bounded cases} drawn from the 2024~Q1 GDELT archive, selected under a pre-specified inclusion protocol across four strata: conflict and shipping disruption (Red Sea, Russia Black Sea), sanctions and export controls (US semiconductor rules, Iran oil sanctions), and commodity supply shocks (China rare-earth curbs, India pharma API disruption). Two cases are examined in depth with full retrieval diagnostics; four additional cases follow the same methodology and contribute descriptive event-study results. For each case, a geographic propagation map traces the event origin through directly affected countries to the Indian sectoral market outcome. It tests direct matching by normalized source URL, lexical candidate generation from GKG metadata, rule-based sector labeling, and descriptive market-model calculations on researcher-defined candidate baskets. These components produce different kinds of evidence. A URL match indicates that records refer to the same source URL; it does not verify the content or truth of an article. A lexical candidate indicates retrieval by metadata overlap; it is not a relevance judgment. A market-model estimate for an analyst-selected basket is descriptive and does not validate the event-to-security mapping.

The results show both an operational capability and a clear retrieval weakness. In the Red Sea case, source matching yielded rows for all 30 selected GDELT records, whereas lexical candidates contained only one exact-source pair and sector rules left 226 of 230 candidates unlabeled. In the semiconductor case, the exact article remained outside the top 50 after query enrichment. Market-response calculations can be produced for selected baskets, but those baskets were not generated or validated by the pipeline. Thus, the prototype can process historical records and preserve traceability while its semantic retrieval and exposure-linking stages remain unvalidated.

This paper makes four bounded contributions:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item it documents an end-to-end historical replay of a proposed geopolitical monitoring workflow across six cases spanning four geopolitical strata;
  \item it reports source-linkage and retrieval diagnostics, including negative results, for two depth cases;
  \item it separates descriptive market calculations from system-derived exposure claims and causal conclusions, using an explicit taxonomy of evidence types (Table~\ref{tab:evidence}); and
  \item it provides a geographic propagation map that traces each case from event origin to Indian sectoral market outcome, making the pipeline's reasoning chain visually inspectable.
\end{enumerate}

Code and outputs are publicly available at \url{https://github.com/krish20041610/GeoSignal}. A longer 2022--2024 daily panel that would support Toda--Yamamoto Granger analysis is left for future work.

The rest of the paper is organized as follows. Section~\ref{sec:related} reviews related work, Section~\ref{sec:system} describes the workflow, Section~\ref{sec:data} introduces the data and cases, Section~\ref{sec:methods} gives the replay methodology, Section~\ref{sec:results} presents the findings, and Sections~\ref{sec:discussion}--\ref{sec:conclusion} discuss implications, limitations, and next steps.

% ------------------------------------------------------------
\section{Background and related work}\label{sec:related}

\subsection{Geopolitical-risk measurement and event data}

Geopolitical risk has been operationalized through news-based measures and structured event records, which offer complementary representations of conflict and political uncertainty~\cite{CaldaraIacoviello2022,PLOVER2023}. Large-scale coded event data such as GDELT~\cite{LeetaruSchrodt2013} provide machine-coded observations of reported events at high frequency. Those records are not a complete or error-free account of geopolitical activity: coding errors, duplicate reporting, and the distinction between media-detection dates and event dates are well-known concerns. GeoSignal therefore treats coded event records as input records and evaluates linkage behavior rather than assuming they are ground truth.

\subsection{Linking events to reporting and economic exposure}

Event extraction and temporal knowledge-graph research provide methods for representing entities, event types, and relations across time~\cite{MAVEN2020,MAVENERE2022,TIMERS2021,KnowEvolve2017,REN2020}. GeoSignal's current replay uses a narrower design: direct URL joins provide source traceability, while TF-IDF~\cite{SaltonBuckley1988} and BM25~\cite{RobertsonZaragoza2009} ranking over GKG metadata supplies additional candidates. This is a retrieval diagnostic, not a semantic event--news model evaluation. In particular, GKG v1 contains metadata fields rather than article bodies, limiting what lexical overlap can establish.

The intended downstream step is to connect event-related entities to sectors or securities. In the current prototype, sector labels rely on deterministic rules, and candidate market baskets are specified by the researcher. Consequently, the case study examines whether these links can be assembled and inspected; it does not establish that the system identifies the economically exposed firms.

\subsection{Market response analysis}

Event studies estimate security returns around dated information events relative to an expected-return model~\cite{MacKinlay1997}. Geopolitical-risk research has also examined heterogeneous responses across sectors and markets~\cite{Choi2024,HassanEtAl2022}. GeoSignal computes market-model estimates for selected baskets to illustrate how market data can be brought into the workflow. Because the baskets are not validated pipeline outputs, the event dates are not isolated interventions, and significance testing was not conducted for these case outputs, the estimates are reported as descriptive context only.

\subsection{Positioning of this work}

Prior work typically evaluates one stage in isolation: risk indices at the aggregate level, event extraction against annotated corpora, or event studies on pre-selected firms. This paper instead reports what happens when the stages are chained and each hand-off is inspected. Its contribution is diagnostic rather than a new model: it shows which links in the chain are supported by direct evidence and which depend on analyst judgment.
The current contribution is narrower than a fully validated news-to-market system: it documents which handoffs in the replay are traceable and where retrieval and exposure mapping fail.

% ------------------------------------------------------------
\section{System architecture}\label{sec:system}

\subsection{Proposed workflow}

GeoSignal is organized as a sequence of analytical stages (Figure~\ref{fig:pipeline}):
\begin{enumerate}
  \item ingest structured geopolitical event records;
  \item retrieve associated news records and retain direct source links where available;
  \item generate additional lexical candidates from news metadata;
  \item extract and map sector-related entities using deterministic rules; and
  \item assemble market data for descriptive event-window analysis.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
