\end{tabular}
\end{table*}

\textbf{CS-03 (Iran sanctions, 16 Feb 2024).}
US Treasury OFAC expanded SDN designations targeting the Iranian oil-trading network. Indian refiners historically sourced discounted Iranian crude; fresh designations forced spot-market substitution and widened the Brent spread. CAMEO root codes 08, 10, 17 and keywords \textit{iran, sanction, ofac, sdn, crude} define the filter.

\textbf{CS-04 (China rare-earth curbs, 01 Feb 2024).}
MOFCOM/GACC enforcement notice tightening export licensing for gallium, germanium, and graphite. India's EV battery and specialty-steel supply chains depend on these inputs. CAMEO root codes 08, 17, 10 and keywords \textit{rare earth, gallium, germanium, graphite, mofcom} define the filter.

\textbf{CS-05 (Russia energy disruption, 18 Jan 2024).}
Escalation of Black Sea maritime attacks disrupted Russian crude tanker corridors. India sources $\approx$40\% of crude from Russia; Black Sea freight premiums rose 54\%. CAMEO root codes 19, 20, 14, 18 and keywords \textit{russia, black sea, crude, tanker, urals} define the filter.

\textbf{CS-06 (India pharma API, 08 Mar 2024).}
Chinese NMPA tightened GMP inspection requirements for API exporters; CDSCO issued an import alert. India imports $\approx$70\% of bulk drug intermediates from China, creating a direct input-cost squeeze on domestic generic manufacturers. CAMEO root codes 08, 17, 10 and keywords \textit{pharma, api, cdsco, nmpa, bulk drug} define the filter.

\subsection{Why these cases}\label{sec:whycases}

The six cases were purposively selected from archived records under the pre-specified inclusion protocol. The two depth cases (CS-01, CS-02) contrast a multi-record conflict sample with a single policy report; the four additional cases extend stratum coverage across sanctions, commodity, and conflict types. All six use the same methodology. Cases are illustrative and were not drawn to be representative (Section~\ref{sec:limitations}). The inclusion protocol and the full case-definition parameters are frozen in \texttt{experiments/case\_studies/case\_registry.py} in the public repository and were not changed after any results were examined.

% ------------------------------------------------------------
\section{Replay methodology}\label{sec:methods}

\subsection{Direct source linkage}

Event and GKG source URLs are canonicalized by lowercasing the host and path, removing a leading \texttt{www.}, omitting the scheme, query, and fragment, and removing trailing path slashes. The case scripts differ slightly: one removes all trailing slashes and the other removes one. URLs are not fetched or checked for redirects. The join is interpreted as source identity evidence only.

\subsection{Lexical candidate generation}

Query text is constructed from event/actor fields and matched against GKG metadata (themes, persons, organisations, and locations). Two standard scorers are used. For TF-IDF, documents and queries are represented as weighted term vectors and ranked by cosine similarity,
\begin{equation}
  \mathrm{score}_{\mathrm{cos}}(q,d) = \frac{\mathbf{w}_q \cdot \mathbf{w}_d}{\lVert \mathbf{w}_q\rVert\,\lVert \mathbf{w}_d\rVert},
  \qquad w_{t,d} = \mathrm{tf}_{t,d}\cdot \log\frac{N}{\mathrm{df}_t}.
\end{equation}
For BM25,
\begin{equation}
  \mathrm{score}_{\mathrm{BM25}}(q,d) = \sum_{t\in q} \mathrm{IDF}(t)\,
  \frac{f_{t,d}\,(k_1+1)}{f_{t,d} + k_1\!\left(1-b+b\,\frac{|d|}{\mathrm{avgdl}}\right)},
\end{equation}
with parameters $k_1=1.5$ and $b=0.75$ in the project implementation. Case~1 uses BM25 top-10 retrieval followed by TF-IDF cosine filtering at 0.05 on same-day records; it does not exercise the configured three-day temporal band. Case~2 ranks all same-day metadata rows with TF-IDF cosine; its reported ranks and cosine score are not BM25 results.

\subsection{Retrieval evaluation}

Evaluation uses exact source-URL identity as a limited silver signal, because no independently labeled relevance set exists. For Case~1 we report (i) the number of candidate pairs and events covered, (ii) the share of candidate pairs that are exact-source pairs, and (iii) the share of events whose exact source appears in the top ten. For Case~2 we report the rank of the exact article in the same-day corpus under a baseline query and under an enriched query that adds semiconductor, advanced-chip, export-control, and Commerce Department terms. A single-rater inspection of the baseline top 20 classified each item as directly relevant, adjacent context, or unrelated using the article title/URL slug and GKG metadata. These labels are diagnostic and do not constitute a broad relevance benchmark.

\subsection{Rule-based sector labeling}

Deterministic entity and keyword rules map retrieved candidates to configured GICS sector categories. Example keywords include crude and LNG for Energy, shipping and Suez for Industrials, rice and wheat for Consumer Staples, and semiconductor and chip for Information Technology. We report label counts, including \emph{Unknown}, as a measure of rule coverage; these text rules are not a validated exposure ontology.

\subsection{Market-model event windows}

For each candidate basket, daily log returns $R_{i,t}$ are regressed on a broad-benchmark return $R_{m,t}$ over a 120-session pre-event estimation window,
\begin{equation}
  R_{i,t} = \alpha_i + \beta_i R_{m,t} + \varepsilon_{i,t}.
\end{equation}
Abnormal returns in the post-event window are $\mathrm{AR}_{i,t} = R_{i,t} - (\hat\alpha_i + \hat\beta_i R_{m,t})$, and cumulative abnormal return over $h$ trading days is (log-return approximation, reported in percent)
\begin{equation}
  \CAR_i(h) = \sum_{t=1}^{h} \mathrm{AR}_{i,t}, \qquad h\in\{1,3,7,14,30\}.
\end{equation}
Benchmarks are the Nifty~50 for the Indian series and the S\&P~500 for the global semiconductor series. Basket membership is an analyst-specified proxy, not an output of GeoSignal's event-to-sector/security mapping. The saved case analyses do not report significance tests; all CARs are therefore treated as descriptive and are not causally attributed to the event.

\subsection{Researcher-defined baskets}

Case~1 uses the researcher-selected ports/logistics basket ADANIPORTS.NS, CONCOR.NS, GESHIP.NS, SCI.NS, and AEGISLOG.NS; energy basket RELIANCE.NS, ONGC.NS, IOC.NS, and BPCL.NS; and FMCG comparison basket HINDUNILVR.NS, ITC.NS, NESTLEIND.NS, and BRITANNIA.NS. The FMCG constituent proxy was used because the sector-index history was unavailable. Case~2 uses global semiconductor firms (NVDA, TSM, ASML, INTC), U.S.\ semiconductor ETFs (SOXX, SMH; context only), and Indian large-cap IT (TCS.NS, INFY.NS, HCLTECH.NS, WIPRO.NS; indirect context). All mappings are researcher-defined and remain unvalidated.

% ------------------------------------------------------------
\section{Results}\label{sec:results}

\subsection{Case 1: Red Sea}

\paragraph{Linkage.} The direct source-URL join produced 31 event/article rows covering all 30 selected event records. The metadata-based stage generated 230 candidate pairs for 25 of the 30 events. Against exact source URLs as the silver linkage signal, one exact-source pair appeared in the lexical candidates (0.43\% of candidate pairs), and the exact source appeared in the top ten for one of 30 events (3.33\%). Combining direct links and lexical candidates yielded 260 unique event/article pairs and a path to a linked record for every selected event. These counts describe path coverage and exact-source overlap, not semantic relevance accuracy.

\paragraph{Scores and sector coverage.} The saved mean and median cosine scores among matched pairs were 0.1818 and 0.1833, respectively, with 9.2 candidate pairs per matched event on average. Rule-based labels assigned Energy to two and Industrials to two of the 230 lexical candidates; the remaining 226 were Unknown. The separate exact-link rows had 29 Unknown and two Consumer Staples labels. An actor-only co-occurrence graph contained nine actor nodes and ten edges, with no sector nodes. These outputs do not supply a validated event-to-sector mapping.

