% ------------------------------------------------------------
\section{Discussion}\label{sec:discussion}

The replay clarifies what a prototype workflow can establish before it has a validated relevance set or exposure taxonomy. Direct URL matching is useful for preserving provenance when the same source appears in both event and news records, but it cannot establish that a report is relevant to an event or that its claims are accurate. Lexical retrieval broadens the candidate set, yet the two case diagnostics show that metadata overlap alone did not reliably rank the exact source near the top. In the Red Sea case, the exact source rarely appeared in lexical candidates; in the semiconductor case, query enrichment improved ranking substantially but did not place the article in the top 50.

\paragraph{Why retrieval is weak.} Several factors plausibly contribute, though this study does not isolate them: GKG v1 lacks article text, so queries built from actor and event fields share few discriminative terms with metadata; same-day corpora of over 100{,}000 rows contain many topically adjacent items; and event records can reference sources whose metadata emphasize different themes than the coded event. Testing these explanations requires ablations (Section~\ref{sec:future}).

\paragraph{Downstream interpretation.} Rule-based sector labels are sparse, while the candidate baskets used for return calculations were selected by the researcher. The calculated CARs are sensitive to the chosen basket and window and may include unrelated market movements. They are not evidence of a system prediction, exposure validation, or causal effect. The six-case cross-section (Table~\ref{tab:crosscase}) and the geographic propagation maps (Section~\ref{sec:propmap}) extend the evidence base across strata, but the same interpretive constraints apply to every case: descriptive market calculations on researcher-defined baskets do not validate the event-to-sector link or establish that a measured return was caused by the event.

\paragraph{Implications for a live system.} A live deployment would inherit the weaknesses documented here. Because direct source links are reliable but sparse, a live monitor should surface provenance and uncertainty explicitly and avoid presenting lexical candidates or sector labels as findings. The replay supports building evaluation infrastructure first, and treating retrieval and exposure mapping as research questions rather than solved components.

% ------------------------------------------------------------
\section{Limitations and threats to validity}\label{sec:limitations}

This study has several substantive limitations.
\emph{Data:} GKG v1 is a metadata source, not article text, so retrieval scores do not measure semantic understanding of full reports; event dates are media-detection dates.
\emph{Sample:} the six cases are purposively selected and not a representative multi-event sample; Case~2 centres on a single article; results do not generalise beyond the 2024~Q1 archive (see Limitation~L-010 in the project documentation).
\emph{Linkage and review:} exact URL identity is only a limited linkage signal, and the semiconductor relevance review is single-rater and small, with no inter-annotator agreement.
\emph{Exposure mapping:} deterministic sector rules have low observed coverage, and no event-to-security mapping is validated.
\emph{Market analysis:} baskets are researcher-defined, the calculations are descriptive, event dates are not isolated interventions, and the case analyses provide no significance test or basis for causal attribution.
\emph{Leontief and temporal analysis:} Leontief propagation results use a synthetic coefficient matrix estimated from GDP shares and are illustrative only (Limitation~L-012). Toda--Yamamoto Granger analysis was not run: the 90-day GDELT archive is insufficient for the required daily time-series length at lag~12 (Limitation~L-009). A longer 2022--2024 panel is left for future work.
\emph{Scope:} the paper evaluates archived replay only; it does not measure live operation, predictive performance, or empirical cross-sector propagation.

% ------------------------------------------------------------
\section{Evaluation roadmap}\label{sec:future}

The findings suggest a practical order for further evaluation.
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Multi-event set with independent relevance judgments.} \textit{Partially addressed:} the registry now covers six cases across four strata (Table~\ref{tab:caseregistry}). The remaining step is at least two independent raters labelling a stratified sample per case and reporting Cohen's kappa, as specified in the case-selection protocol.
  \item \textbf{Retrieval ablations.} Compare TF-IDF versus BM25, query construction variants, metadata-field subsets, and, where licensing allows, full-text or embedding-based retrieval on held-out cases.
  \item \textbf{Exposure validation.} Define an explicit sector/security taxonomy, pre-specify event-to-exposure links before looking at returns, and measure mapping precision and coverage. The two-rater exposure-link review specified in the protocol (§8 of \texttt{docs/DECISIONS.md}) is a prerequisite before any market claim is strengthened.
  \item \textbf{Temporal analysis on a longer panel.} Expand the GDELT archive to 2022--2024 (batch downloader available in the repository) and run the pre-specified Toda--Yamamoto Granger analysis. Do not run this on the 90-day dev panel; the test is underpowered at lag~12 with fewer than 48 observations.
  \item \textbf{Market-response evaluation.} Only after items 1--3, evaluate market-response or predictive claims using appropriate benchmarks, multiple events, clustered or otherwise robust inference, and uncertainty estimates.
  \item \textbf{Leontief with an empirical I-O matrix.} Replace the synthetic GDP-share matrix with the India MoSPI 2017-18 or WIOD 2016 table. The \texttt{io\_loader.py} module in the repository automates the WIOD download and aggregation.
  \item \textbf{Event-time verification.} Check event dates against reporting and primary sources rather than relying solely on media-detection dates.
\end{enumerate}

% ------------------------------------------------------------
\section{Reproducibility and availability}\label{sec:repro}

GDELT event and GKG exports are publicly available. The archived event and GKG inputs cover 1 January--31 March 2024; the market-price request covers 1 June 2023--30 April 2024. The analysis uses deterministic procedures and no random seed.

\paragraph{Code and data availability.}
The full GeoSignal pipeline, all six case study scripts, the geographic propagation map module, and the outputs underlying every number in Section~\ref{sec:results} are publicly available at:

\begin{center}
\url{https://github.com/krish20041610/GeoSignal}
\end{center}

The repository includes \texttt{run\_all.py} (end-to-end pipeline runner), \texttt{experiments/case\_studies/run\_all\_cases.py} (six-case runner with cross-case summary table and LaTeX output), and \texttt{src/geosignal/visualization/propagation\_map.py} (geographic map generator). Raw GDELT and GKG ZIPs are not committed (file size); they are re-downloadable deterministically from the GDELT public archive using the included downloader. The \texttt{README.md} documents every step required to reproduce the outputs from scratch.

\paragraph{Ethics and responsible use.}
GeoSignal processes public news metadata and public market data; it does not involve human subjects. Outputs are research diagnostics, not trading or investment advice. The author declares no external funding and no conflicts of interest related to this work.

% ------------------------------------------------------------
\section{Conclusion}\label{sec:conclusion}

This paper reports an offline historical replay of selected stages in GeoSignal, a prototype proposed for future geopolitical monitoring. Across six 2024~Q1 cases spanning four geopolitical strata, direct source matching preserved traceable links while metadata-based retrieval performed weakly against exact-source diagnostics and rule-based sector labeling remained sparse. Descriptive market-model estimates were computed for researcher-defined baskets, and geographic propagation maps trace each case from event origin through affected countries to the Indian sectoral market outcome. Neither the market estimates nor the maps establish causal exposure: they document the reasoning chain the pipeline produced and identify where that chain depends on analyst judgment rather than validated system output. A longer 2022--2024 daily panel supporting Toda--Yamamoto Granger analysis and a two-rater exposure-link review are the next concrete steps before any stronger monitoring or predictive claim can be made. Code, case scripts, and all outputs are publicly available at \url{https://github.com/krish20041610/GeoSignal}.

